om2
note SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
note bridge/shlib/shlib.om: writes an AArch64 Linux shared library (ELF64 ET_DYN) from a table and the native
note object omc emits for an OM-2 unit with `export fn` entries, and a probe executable that loads it through the
note system's dynamic loader and calls it. No C compiler, assembler or linker is involved.
note Build: seed/out/omc < bridge/shlib/shlib.om > shlib
note   shlib lib TABLE OBJECT OUT   the library: DT_SONAME from TABLE, one global function symbol per export row
note   shlib call TABLE OUT         the probe: an executable that needs the TABLE's SONAME (searched next to the
note                                probe, DT_RUNPATH $ORIGIN, bound at load), makes each call row's call in order
note                                and exits 0 when every result matches, 1+k (100 for k >= 99) when call k does
note                                not, 250 when x19..x27, x29, d8..d15 or the stack pointer did not come back
note                                unchanged
note OBJECT is omc's output for the line "object" followed by the unit (OMOBJ001, spec/omegese-modules.md).
note TABLE: one row per line, fields separated by one tab; an empty line or one starting with # is skipped.
note   soname NAME               the library's DT_SONAME (exactly one row)
note   needed NAME               a DT_NEEDED entry: a library the dynamic linker loads first (any number, in order)
note   runpath PATH              the library's DT_RUNPATH, where its needed libraries are searched (at most one;
note                             $ORIGIN is the library's own directory)
note   state NAME BYTES          a zero-filled writable region of BYTES bytes (1..4294967296), 64-byte aligned,
note                             in the library's data segment (one copy per process); a state row comes before
note                             every row that names it
note   lock STATE LOCK UNLOCK    every frame export (below) calls the C function LOCK with STATE's address first
note                             and UNLOCK with it on both its return and its trap path (at most one row; LOCK
note                             and UNLOCK, pthread_mutex_lock and pthread_mutex_unlock for example, are imports
note                             of the library); LOCK answering nonzero returns TRAP without running the body
note   import NAME MAP...        how the C arguments of the object's import NAME are made from omc's parameter
note                             slots: up to 8 tokens sK (slot K) or sK+N (slot K plus the byte offset N); without
note                             a row, C argument k is slot k
note   export NAME TRAP          NAME, an `export fn` of the object, becomes a global function symbol; a trap
note                             inside it returns TRAP (0..4294967295) to the C caller
note   export NAME TRAP frame STATE...   the same, as a frame export: omc's first parameter is a sized view or
note                             edit of ulong (count 1..9 + states) holding the C argument registers x0..x7 as
note                             they came (fr 0..7), then the address of every state row in table order (fr 8
note                             on), then the address of the caller's stack arguments (fr 8 + states: AAPCS64
note                             argument 9 on, 8 bytes each, a narrow one in its low bytes with the rest
note                             unspecified); each further parameter, one per STATE named (at most 5), is a sized
note                             view or edit that the wrapper points at that state, at most its size
note   call NAME EXPECT ARG...   the probe calls NAME with up to 10 arguments (a decimal 64-bit value, buf for
note                             a pointer to one writable buffer, every byte 0xA5 at load and shared by every
note                             call, so a later call reads what an earlier one wrote, buf+N for the pointer
note                             N bytes into it, or bufword for the u64 that buffer holds at offset 0;
note                             arguments 9 and 10 go on the stack, as AAPCS64
note                             passes them) and compares the low 32 bits of the result with EXPECT (any:
note                             not compared, for an address)
note   bufeq OFF VALUE           the probe compares the u64 the buffer holds at byte offset OFF with VALUE, all
note                             64 bits, after the rows before it (at least one call row)
note   bufset OFF VALUE          the probe stores the u64 VALUE at byte offset OFF of the buffer, after the rows
note                             before it (at least one call row), so a later call reads it
note   reteq OFF VALUE           the probe compares the u64 at byte OFF from the address the last call returned
note                             with VALUE (at least one call row before it)
note The buffer reaches 4096 bytes past the largest offset buf+N, bufeq or bufset names.
note From the C calling convention (AAPCS64) to omc's function ABI, per export: each C argument register is one
note omc parameter slot, in order (a scalar or a sized array: one slot; an unsized view or edit: a pointer slot
note then a ulong count slot). Narrow integer arguments are sign- or zero-extended as omc expects. x7 points at a
note per-call context on the caller's stack whose byte offset 16 ([x7, #16]) holds the trap entry; x19..x30 and d8..d15 are saved
note there and restored on both the return and the trap path. The result is returned in x0.
note Imports (an `extern fn` of the object): each becomes an undefined global function symbol bound at load
note through a GOT slot (R_AARCH64_GLOB_DAT), and omc's BL to it goes to a veneer in .text that saves x7 and x30
note (omc's context and return; C may change x7), makes the C arguments (the import row), calls through the
note GOT, extends a narrow result as omc expects (C leaves the upper bits undefined) and returns. omc saves its
note own live registers around the BL. An import's stack need counts as 0: the C callee runs on the caller's
note stack below omc's frames, as any C call does.
note Refused (exit 1, nothing written), each with its own message: an object that is not an OMOBJ001 version 1
note AArch64 object (header, trailing bytes, records, padding, names); an import with an address or a stack
note need; a function address
note outside the code, unaligned or out of order, or an export's inside the entry slots; an entry slot that does not branch to
note its function; a BL with no call record or two; a call record whose offset, source or cost does not fit its
note BL, or whose destination is not the BL's target (an import's BL is the unlinked word 0x94000000); a
note base-register use of x7 other than the trap-entry load
note [x7, #16] (a pattern check over the load/store group); a stack need above the 2^48-byte address space or a
note call cycle; a float scalar parameter or result (omc passes them in x registers, C in v registers); more than 6
note parameter slots; a table export that is not an export of the object; a repeated or malformed name; a
note malformed row or number; no soname or no export; a call of a name that is no export of the table; an
note import row naming no import of the object, or a slot past the import's slots; a frame export whose
note parameters do not match its row; a lock with an export that is not a frame export.
note Exit 0 written, 1 refused, 2 usage, 3 a file unreadable or unwritable or memory refused.
note Every buffer is a run-time pool sized from its input. The wrappers, entry slots and probe reach their
note targets with ADR and LDR literal (1 MiB) and B and BL (128 MiB) because each is one word, the data segment
note with ADRP (4 GiB); an output past their reach is refused (e_reach), never truncated. That is a choice of
note instruction forms, not a machine limit: a larger output needs longer sequences and veneers for far branches.
note A frame export's frame (72 + 8 per state bytes, 16-aligned) is reached with 12-bit immediates, and so is the
note caller's stack 192 bytes above it (e_reach when frame + 192 passes 4080).
global ib uchar *
global eb uchar *
global ds uchar *
global ex ulong *
global cl ulong *
global cd ulong *
global fr ulong *
global ck ulong *
global st ulong *
global im ulong *
global mp ulong *
global nd ulong *
global ag uchar 4096
global fo ulong 16
global fl ulong 16
global nv ulong 64
global ly ulong 32
note nv: 0 table end (object start), 1 object end, 2 dynstr length, 3 exports, 4 calls, 5 soname offset in ib,
note 6 soname length, 7 functions, 8 code end, 9 fields in the current row, 10 output length, 13 soname in
note dynstr, 14 runpath in dynstr, 15 section header offset, 16 address minus offset of the current section,
note 17 object call records, 18 a stack need changed in this pass, 19 the previous function address,
note 20 and 21 the slot and extension counts of exts, 22 the object's exported functions, 23 states, 24 import
note symbols, 25 import rows, 26 needed rows, 27 the runpath's offset in ib plus 1 (0 none), 28 its length,
note 29 the lock state plus 1 (0 none), 30 and 31 the LOCK name in ib, 32 and 33 the UNLOCK name, 34 and 35
note their import symbols, 36 the state area's size, 37 .dynamic bytes, 38 the shared return's bytes
note ck, per object call record (4): source function, destination function, stack cost, instruction offset
note SMAX: the most stack a call may need, the AArch64 Linux user address space (48-bit virtual addresses,
note 2^48 bytes): no stack can be larger. Every settled need and cost is at most SMAX, so need + cost stays
note below 2^64; the probe's page count (at most 2^36) takes MOVZ and two MOVKs.
const SMAX ulong 281474976710656
note ex, per export (EXW): 0 name offset in ib, 1 name length, 2 trap value, 3 function index, 4 dynstr offset,
note 5 wrapper offset in .text, 6 wrapper bytes, 7 extensions, 8 1 for a frame export, 9 its states, 10..14
note those states, 15 its frame bytes
const EXW ulong 16
note st, per state (4): 0 name offset in ib, 1 name length, 2 bytes, 3 offset in the state area
note im, per import symbol (8): 0 name offset in ib, 1 name length, 2 dynstr offset, 3 object function (the
note function count when only the lock calls it), 4 import row (the row count when none), 5 veneer offset in
note .text, 6 veneer bytes
note mp, per import row (20): 0 name offset in ib, 1 name length, 2 tokens, 3..10 their slots, 11..18 their
note offsets, 19 1 once an import uses the row
note nd, per needed row (3): 0 name offset in ib, 1 name length, 2 dynstr offset
note cl, per call row (CLW): 0 name offset, 1 name length, 2 expect, 3 arguments, 4..13 values, 14..23 1 for buf,
note 2 for bufword, 24 1 for a bufeq row (its offset in 4, its value in 5; its name the call row's before it)
const CLW ulong 25
note cd, per call: the dynstr offset of its name
note fr, per object function (8): 0 flags, 1 address, 2 result type, 3 parameters, 4 parameter records offset
note in ib, 5 name offset in ib, 6 name length
text m_lib "lib"
text m_call "call"
text k_son "soname"
text k_exp "export"
text k_call "call"
text k_buf "buf"
text k_bufw "bufword"
text k_bufeq "bufeq"
text k_bufp "buf+"
text k_bufset "bufset"
text k_reteq "reteq"
text k_any "any"
text k_need "needed"
text k_rpath "runpath"
text k_state "state"
text k_lock "lock"
text k_imp "import"
text k_frame "frame"
text k_obj "OMOBJ001"
text t_interp "/lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1"
text t_origin "$ORIGIN"
note the section names, each space written as a zero: .hash 1, .dynsym 7, .dynstr 15, .text 23, .dynamic 29,
note .rela.dyn 38, .got 48, .bss 53, .shstrtab 58
text t_shs " .hash .dynsym .dynstr .text .dynamic .rela.dyn .got .bss .shstrtab "
text e_usage "usage: shlib lib TABLE OBJECT OUT | shlib call TABLE OUT\n"
text e_read "shlib: cannot read an input file\n"
text e_write "shlib: cannot write the output file\n"
text e_mem "shlib: memory refused\n"
text e_row "shlib: malformed table row\n"
text e_num "shlib: malformed number in the table\n"
text e_name "shlib: a name outside A-Z a-z 0-9 _ (1..63 bytes; a soname also . + -, any length) or repeated\n"
text e_son "shlib: the table needs exactly one soname row\n"
text e_noexp "shlib: the table names no export\n"
text e_trap "shlib: a trap value above 4294967295\n"
text e_obj "shlib: the object is not an OMOBJ001 version 1 AArch64 object\n"
text e_imp "shlib: an import with an address or a stack need\n"
text e_improw "shlib: an import row names no import of the object, or a slot past the import's slots, or more than 8\n"
text e_frame "shlib: a frame export's parameters do not match its row (a sized ulong frame of 1..9 + states, then one sized view or edit per state, within its size)\n"
text e_state "shlib: a state row is malformed, repeated, or named before it is declared\n"
text e_lock "shlib: a lock row is malformed or repeated, or an export of a locked library is not a frame export\n"
text e_rt "shlib: the code loads a runtime entry other than the trap entry\n"
text e_rtw "shlib: the code reads or writes through the per-call context (x7) other than the trap-entry load\n"
text e_addr "shlib: a function address is outside the code, unaligned, out of order or inside the entry slots\n"
text e_slotb "shlib: an export's entry slot does not branch to its function\n"
text e_bl "shlib: a BL word has no call record, or two records name one BL\n"
text e_call "shlib: a call record's offset, source or cost does not fit its BL\n"
text e_cdst "shlib: a call record's destination is not its BL's target\n"
text e_miss "shlib: a table export is not an export of the object\n"
text e_flt "shlib: a float parameter or result; refused\n"
text e_slot "shlib: more than 6 parameter slots\n"
text e_ncall "shlib: a call names no export of the table\n"
text e_reach "shlib: output past the AArch64 branch or literal reach\n"
text e_stk "shlib: a function needs more stack than the 2^48-byte address space, or its calls form a cycle\n"
note ---- small helpers
fn die t view uchar * code int to void
r = write 2 t 0 len t
exit code
end
fn al8 x ulong to ulong
ret and add x 7 not ulong 7
end
fn al16 x ulong to ulong
ret and add x 15 not ulong 15
end
fn w8 o ulong v ulong to void
put eb o uchar and v 255
end
fn w16 o ulong v ulong to void
w8 o v
w8 add o 1 shr v 8
end
fn w32 o ulong v ulong to void
w16 o and v 65535
w16 add o 2 shr v 16
end
fn w64 o ulong v ulong to void
w32 o and v 4294967295
w32 add o 4 shr v 32
end
note the u32 of eb at o
fn r32 o ulong to ulong
v = ulong 0
for i ulong 4
v = or v shl ulong get eb add o i mul i 8
end
ret v
end
note the u64 of ib at o, inside the object
fn q o ulong to ulong
if gt add o 8 get nv 1
die e_obj 1
end
v = ulong 0
for i ulong 8
v = or v shl ulong get ib add o i mul i 8
end
ret v
end
note the u64 at object offset o
fn oq o ulong to ulong
ret q add get nv 0 o
end
note argument ai into ag; its length
fn ap ai ulong to ulong
r = argread ai ag 0 4095
if le r 0
die e_usage 2
end
ret ulong r
end
fn argis ai ulong t view uchar * to bool
r = argread ai ag 0 4095
if ne r long len t
ret false
end
for i ulong len t bound 4096
if ne get ag i get t i
ret false
end
end
ret true
end
note append argument ai's file to ib from offset at; the new end
fn ldf ai ulong from ulong to ulong
m = ap ai
fd = fopen ag 0 m int 524288 int 0
if lt fd 0
die e_read 3
end
n = from
while true bound 9223372036854775807
if lt sub len ib n 65536
r = enlarge ib add add n 65536 len ib
if lt r 0
die e_mem 3
end
end
x = read int fd ib n sub len ib n
if lt x 0
die e_read 3
end
if eq x 0
break
end
n = add n ulong x
end
r = fclose int fd
ret n
end
note write eb[0, nv 10) to argument ai's path with mode md
fn save ai ulong md int to void
m = ap ai
r = unlink ag 0 m
fd = fopen ag 0 m int 524865 md
if lt fd 0
die e_write 3
end
n = get nv 10
o = ulong 0
while lt o n bound 9223372036854775807
x = write int fd eb o sub n o
if le x 0
die e_write 3
end
o = add o ulong x
end
r = fclose int fd
if lt r 0
die e_write 3
end
end
note eb grown to n bytes, all zero
fn ebnew n ulong to void
if gt n len eb
r = enlarge eb n
if lt r 0
die e_mem 3
end
end
for i ulong n bound 9223372036854775807
put eb i 0
end
put nv 10 n
end
note ---- table rows: the fields of one line into fo/fl, their count into nv 9; returns the next line
fn row o ulong e ulong to ulong
put nv 9 0
s = o
p = o
while lt p e bound 9223372036854775807
c = get ib p
if eq c 10
break
end
if eq c 9
k = get nv 9
if ge k 15
die e_row 1
end
put fo k s
put fl k sub p s
put nv 9 add k 1
s = add p 1
end
p = add p 1
end
k = get nv 9
put fo k s
put fl k sub p s
put nv 9 add k 1
if lt p e
p = add p 1
end
ret p
end
fn ft i ulong t view uchar * to bool
if ne get fl i len t
ret false
end
o = get fo i
for j ulong len t bound 4096
if ne get ib add o j get t j
ret false
end
end
ret true
end
note field i starts with text t and is longer
fn fpre i ulong t view uchar * to bool
if le get fl i len t
ret false
end
o = get fo i
for j ulong len t bound 4096
if ne get ib add o j get t j
ret false
end
end
ret true
end
note field i as a decimal u64
fn fnum i ulong to ulong
ret dnum get fo i get fl i
end
note ib[o, o+n) as a decimal u64
fn dnum o ulong n ulong to ulong
if or eq n 0 gt n 20
die e_num 1
end
v = ulong 0
for j ulong n bound 20
c = get ib add o j
if or lt c 48 gt c 57
die e_num 1
end
d = ulong sub c 48
if gt v div sub 18446744073709551615 d 10
die e_num 1
end
v = add mul v 10 d
end
ret v
end
note ib[a, a+n) equals ib[b, b+n)
fn same a ulong b ulong n ulong to bool
for j ulong n bound 9223372036854775807
if ne get ib add a j get ib add b j
ret false
end
end
ret true
end
note field i is a name: 1..63 bytes of A-Z a-z 0-9 _ (omc's own name limit), or when so (a soname) at least
note one byte of those and . + -
fn fname i ulong so bool to void
n = get fl i
if or eq n 0 and not so gt n 63
die e_name 1
end
o = get fo i
for j ulong n bound 9223372036854775807
c = get ib add o j
ok = or and ge c 48 le c 57 eq c 95
ok = or ok or and ge c 65 le c 90 and ge c 97 le c 122
if so
ok = or ok or or eq c 46 eq c 43 eq c 45
end
if not ok
die e_name 1
end
end
end
note the export named ib[a, a+n), or the export count when none
fn exfind a ulong n ulong to ulong
nx = get nv 3
for k ulong nx bound 9223372036854775807
b = mul k EXW
if eq get ex add b 1 n
if same get ex b a n
ret k
end
end
end
ret nx
end
fn exrow to void
nf = get nv 9
if lt nf 3
die e_row 1
end
nb = ulong 0
if gt nf 3
if not ft 3 k_frame
die e_row 1
end
nb = sub nf 4
if gt nb 5
die e_frame 1
end
end
fname 1 false
nx = get nv 3
if ne exfind get fo 1 get fl 1 nx
die e_name 1
end
t = fnum 2
if gt t 4294967295
die e_trap 1
end
r = enlarge ex mul add nx 1 EXW
if lt r 0
die e_mem 3
end
b = mul nx EXW
for j ulong EXW
put ex add b j 0
end
put ex b get fo 1
put ex add b 1 get fl 1
put ex add b 2 t
if gt nf 3
put ex add b 8 1
end
put ex add b 9 nb
for j ulong nb bound 5
s = stfind add j 4
if eq s get nv 23
die e_state 1
end
put ex add add b 10 j s
end
put nv 3 add nx 1
end
note the state named by field i, or the state count when none
fn stfind i ulong to ulong
n = get nv 23
for k ulong n bound 9223372036854775807
b = mul k 4
if eq get st add b 1 get fl i
if same get st b get fo i get fl i
ret k
end
end
end
ret n
end
fn strow to void
if ne get nv 9 3
die e_state 1
end
fname 1 false
n = get nv 23
if ne stfind 1 n
die e_state 1
end
z = fnum 2
if or eq z 0 gt z 4294967296
die e_state 1
end
r = enlarge st mul add n 1 4
if lt r 0
die e_mem 3
end
b = mul n 4
put st b get fo 1
put st add b 1 get fl 1
put st add b 2 z
put st add b 3 get nv 36
put nv 36 add get nv 36 and add z 63 not ulong 63
put nv 23 add n 1
end
fn needrow to void
if ne get nv 9 2
die e_row 1
end
fname 1 true
n = get nv 26
r = enlarge nd mul add n 1 3
if lt r 0
die e_mem 3
end
b = mul n 3
put nd b get fo 1
put nd add b 1 get fl 1
put nd add b 2 0
put nv 26 add n 1
end
fn rprow to void
if or ne get nv 9 2 ne get nv 27 0
die e_row 1
end
n = get fl 1
if eq n 0
die e_row 1
end
o = get fo 1
for j ulong n bound 9223372036854775807
if eq get ib add o j 0
die e_row 1
end
end
put nv 27 add o 1
put nv 28 n
end
fn lockrow to void
if or ne get nv 9 4 ne get nv 29 0
die e_lock 1
end
k = stfind 1
if eq k get nv 23
die e_state 1
end
fname 2 false
fname 3 false
put nv 29 add k 1
put nv 30 get fo 2
put nv 31 get fl 2
put nv 32 get fo 3
put nv 33 get fl 3
end
note the import row named ib[a, a+n), or the row count when none
fn mpfind a ulong n ulong to ulong
m = get nv 25
for k ulong m bound 9223372036854775807
b = mul k 20
if eq get mp add b 1 n
if same get mp b a n
ret k
end
end
end
ret m
end
fn improw to void
nf = get nv 9
if or lt nf 2 gt nf 10
die e_improw 1
end
fname 1 false
m = get nv 25
if ne mpfind get fo 1 get fl 1 m
die e_name 1
end
r = enlarge mp mul add m 1 20
if lt r 0
die e_mem 3
end
b = mul m 20
put mp b get fo 1
put mp add b 1 get fl 1
nt = sub nf 2
put mp add b 2 nt
put mp add b 19 0
for j ulong nt bound 8
mtok add j 2 add b j
end
put nv 25 add m 1
end
note field i, a token sK or sK+N, into mp at slot word w + 3 and offset word w + 11
fn mtok i ulong w ulong to void
o = get fo i
n = get fl i
if or lt n 2 ne get ib o 115
die e_improw 1
end
p = ulong 1
while lt p n bound 9223372036854775807
if eq get ib add o p 43
break
end
p = add p 1
end
put mp add w 3 dnum add o 1 sub p 1
v = ulong 0
if lt p n
v = dnum add add o p 1 sub sub n p 1
end
put mp add w 11 v
end
note one table row after soname
fn rowx to void
if ft 0 k_exp
exrow
ret
end
if ft 0 k_call
callrow
ret
end
if ft 0 k_bufeq
eqrow 1
ret
end
if ft 0 k_bufset
eqrow 2
ret
end
if ft 0 k_reteq
eqrow 3
ret
end
if ft 0 k_need
needrow
ret
end
if ft 0 k_rpath
rprow
ret
end
if ft 0 k_state
strow
ret
end
if ft 0 k_lock
lockrow
ret
end
if ft 0 k_imp
improw
ret
end
die e_row 1
end
fn callrow to void
nf = get nv 9
if or lt nf 3 gt nf 13
die e_row 1
end
fname 1 false
b = clnew
put cl b get fo 1
put cl add b 1 get fl 1
e = ulong 4294967296
if not ft 2 k_any
e = fnum 2
end
if and gt e 4294967295 not ft 2 k_any
die e_num 1
end
put cl add b 2 e
na = sub nf 3
put cl add b 3 na
for j ulong na bound 10
f = add j 3
if ft f k_buf
put cl add add b 14 j 1
else
if ft f k_bufw
put cl add add b 14 j 2
else
if fpre f k_bufp
put cl add add b 14 j 1
u = dnum add get fo f 4 sub get fl f 4
bufref u
put cl add add b 4 j u
else
put cl add add b 4 j fnum f
end
end
end
end
end
note a bufeq (kind 1), bufset (2) or reteq (3) row after a call row, whose name it takes (its GOT slot is never
note called)
fn eqrow kind ulong to void
nc = get nv 4
if or ne get nv 9 3 eq nc 0
die e_row 1
end
o = fnum 1
if ne kind 3
bufref o
end
v = fnum 2
a = mul sub nc 1 CLW
b = clnew
put cl b get cl a
put cl add b 1 get cl add a 1
put cl add b 4 o
put cl add b 5 v
put cl add b 24 kind
end
note the buffer reaches 4096 bytes past the largest offset a row names (nv 39 holds that offset + 1)
fn bufref o ulong to void
if gt o sub SMAX 1
die e_num 1
end
if ge o get nv 39
put nv 39 add o 1
end
end
note a new zeroed call row; its first word
